p=['<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><link rel="icon" href="data:,"><title>Glass Table · one consistent learning experience</title><style>'+css+'</style></head><body><nav aria-label="Report sections"><a href="#direction">Direction</a><a href="#impact">Whole-app impact</a><a href="#compare">Before / after</a><a href="#rules">Shared rules</a><a href="#voice">Tone & language</a><a href="#research">Research</a><a href="#decisions">Decisions</a><a href="#testing">Testing</a></nav><main><div class="eyebrow">GLASS TABLE / OCTOBER 10, 2026 / EXPLORATION ONLY</div><h1>One app, from the first hand to the next session</h1><p class="lead">Keep the approved experience-first opening. Refine its visual and interaction rules to match the whole app, then make the same teaching promise in every mode.</p><p>You approved the proposals and asked us to broaden the scope before building. This report explores that fit. <strong>No Swift changes, app build, simulator launch, data reset or installation.</strong> Your installed app remains 1.0 (19).</p><p class="note">Source audit: native a3db5fc / documentation HEAD 65faf87. Before images are retained native fixtures from the build 18/19 galleries, with per-frame provenance; after images are new browser proposals. This is a representative whole-app pattern study, not a fresh exhaustive native screen crawl.</p>']

p += [section('My recommendation', '''<div class="callout"><strong>Adapt the opening to the native system; share the learning behavior across the app.</strong><p>Preserve the current warm palette, Pretendard fonts, native button families and stable bottom actions. Let onboarding be welcoming, practice be concise, progress be factual and settings be functional. They should feel related without being forced into one screen template.</p></div><div class="three"><article class="card"><div class="tag">Preserve</div><h3>The recognizable foundation</h3><p>Warm paper / charcoal, amber actions, green results, fixed green tables, consistent card ownership, open lesson choice, help-neutral progress and compact settings.</p></article><article class="card"><div class="tag">Adapt the proposal</div><h3>Use native geometry and type</h3><p>Replace the opening trial’s 52-pixel default and system font with the native 54-point primary minimum and Pretendard. Keep 60-point controls as a comparison, not a new global rule. Remove teal as the default action-color candidate.</p></article><article class="card"><div class="tag">Refine across modes</div><h3>Make behavior predictable</h3><p>One clear task, visible help, stable answer order, source-preserving returns and a reason after each meaningful decision. Keep chart/model/game feedback distinct.</p></article></div><p><a href="../2026-10-10-first-five-minutes/index.html">Approved opening research and alternatives</a> · <a href="../../build-history.md">Build / feedback history</a> · <a href="../../research/2026-10-10-whole-app-consistency.md">Primary-source research notes</a></p>''','direction')]

p += [section('How the opening changes the rest of the app', table(['Area','Current foundation / what works','Refinement and compromise','Risk or consequence'],[
('First run','Five required comparisons; placement affects recommendations, not mastery.','Teach one pair rule first, explain, then a second comparison. Optional familiarity afterward. Keep the remaining three categories visible.','A two-example checkpoint is exposure, not completion. Resume and routing must be specified before native work.'),
('Learn & Play roots','Stable four tabs, open lesson choice, distinct free and graded modes.','Name destinations by intent: learn a rule, practice decisions, play a game. Use the same Continue-learning phrase for resume.','Do not replace mode selection with an obligatory onboarding replay or create two competing resume destinations.'),
('Worked guides','Learner-paced examples, paired text, boxed seat loop and chart primer.','One rule or relationship per stage; Previous always returns to the preceding teaching step. Keep essential explanations on screen until the learner advances.','Fewer words must not omit blind definitions, turn order, chart axes or contribution arithmetic.'),
('Lesson questions','Equal choices, one-tap answers, compact reopenable cues.','Put situation and decision above the dock. Keep help visibly available, then show rule + why after commit.','No answer-revealing color or selected-hand recommendation before the learner commits.'),
('Feedback','App voice and separate answer labels already exist.','Correct: one result and one reason. Wrong: your answer and correct answer in separate rows, then the reason. Unknown: explanation without a public penalty badge.','Match labels to semantics; no “I win / You win” duplication or praise that contradicts the answer.'),
('Graded 1 vs 1','Chart access and compact choices already exist; chart / EV basis are disclosed.','Use stable card, pot, call and turn context; one takeaway first; chart and opponent-rule access available throughout.','Chart agreement is not perfect poker. After shared cards, the no-later-bets model remains explicit.'),
('Free Play & Quick Play','Concrete help labels, compact names, exit/new-game paths and factual review.','Preserve the table metaphor; teach terms with brief contextual help. Show an end-session sentence plus exact denominators and hand review.','Winning does not prove a good decision. Do not label bluff/luck/safe-risk behavior unless its definition and data support the claim.'),
('Progress','Activity calendar, completion and collapsed detailed records already lead.','Connect “examples explored” to a useful next topic, and keep practice-date and completion metrics separate.','Worked examples and Free Play currently do not count as recorded practice dates. Do not silently change that definition.'),
('Settings & recovery','Native toggles, compact menus, sound/haptics and privacy controls.','Group by learning, feedback, preferences and data. Keep language/appearance lower; use menu values rather than a fake binary toggle.','System / Light / Dark is three states. Delete/restore needs exact consequences, not encouraging lesson copy.')
])+'<p>The opening promises “you can learn one decision and understand why.” Every mode should deliver that promise, while preserving the different purposes and scoring rules above.</p>','impact')]

p += [section('Apply the same rules to every lesson family',table(['Lesson family','What should be visible first','Support and explanation'],[
('Who wins?','Cards, ownership and the comparison rule.','Highlight relevant cards only after commit; explain pair, rank or five-card category.'),
('Which cards turn it around?','Current hands/board and a visual card choice.','Group equivalent ranks where suits have the same consequence; preserve suit-specific exceptions. Explain the new winning category.'),
('Count the pot','Actor, chips added now and cumulative action history.','Teach blinds first; distinguish added chips from total raised to. Reveal the arithmetic after the final answer.'),
('Who acts first?','Numbered portrait seat loop and current street.','Show direction and dealer/blinds before acronyms. Keep teaching in content, Previous/Next in the action area.'),
('Should I enter first?','Cards and plain hand description.','Teach suited/offsuit and chart axes before notation such as K6s. Offer the chart throughout.'),
('An opponent raised','Cards, opponent action and what each choice does.','Keep voluntary chart support and varied strong/weak hands; explain chart policy after commit.'),
('Equity sense','A visual situation and the quantity being estimated.','Teach share of the pot, including ties, before asking for a percentage. Do not call equity simply win probability.'),
('Pot odds / call-or-fold practice','Pot, cost to call and the precise decision.','Use one-tap choices, with an expandable break-even calculation and assumptions. Do not expose a quiz total before commitment.'),
('Reading ranges / actions','Observed action and possible opponent hands.','Plain descriptions first; chart/range evidence and policy accessible. Keep missing/extra hands understandable after an answer.'),
('Optional Go deeper','Explicit advanced-topic invitation.','Retain exact counts, notation and model mathematics for people who want them. Do not put advanced material back in the first-run path.'),
('Guided hand / mixed review','Current turn, stage and a clear next action.','Explain terms in context; later questions need fresh deals and reasons. Exposure, review credit and completion keep their existing definitions.')
])+'<p>This is a consistency contract across lesson families, not a new content bank or a claim that every native branch has been visually retested. The current track suggestion already brings outs early; every lesson remains open. Keep optional depth rather than deleting the app’s mathematical substance.</p>')]

